=== REASONING ===

Why the design preserves ADR-015 + P1-P10 + the non-AI baseline:

P1 (provider port):     Strategy never imports provider/* directly; the
                        handler resolves *provider.Registry.For("drive-coaching")
                        on every request, so a future provider swap is one
                        registry edit. Same as anomaly-explanations and nl-search.

P2 (deny-by-default):   guard.Wrap("drive-coaching", …) returns 404 when
                        ai_mode='off' OR the per-feature toggle is off. The
                        load-bearing TestDriveCoachingAIOffShowsOnlyBaselineStats
                        (Go) probes the route with mode='off' AND toggle=true to
                        defeat the trivial-pass shortcut.

P3 (per-feature opt-in): registry "drive-coaching" entry has DefaultOn:false. The
                        React gate also requires ai_features['drive-coaching']:true
                        (proven by the 2nd vitest case: mode='cloud' but
                        toggle=false → component absent).

P4 (strategy/dispatch loop): The handler runs dispatch.New(tools, prov,
                        denyAllConfirm, maxIters=6).Run(ctx, strategy, input,
                        sseW). The dispatcher enforces tool whitelisting against
                        the strategy's Tools() list and HALTS at the first
                        non-allowed tool name; mutating tools (none in this
                        slice) would be denied at confirm-time.

P5 (decorator chain):   provider.Registry.For wires
                            redact → rate-limit → cost-cap → audit → trace
                        in the locked order. The handler sees only the fully
                        decorated provider; no decorator is bypassed.

P6 (backend guard +     ai_routes.go: r.Post("/drives/{driveID}/coach",
   frontend HOC):           g.Wrap("drive-coaching", driveCoachHandler))
                        AIDriveCoaching = withAiFeature('drive-coaching',
                                                       InnerSection)
                        Both gates are anchored to the same string ID; aivet's
                        cross-check passes (13/11/sync).

P7 (typed DTOs only):   The tool input is `{"drive_id": int64}`; the output
                        envelope is a single typed struct
                        (DriveTelemetrySummary) with explicit *float64 nullables
                        for every optional aggregate. Validate rejects missing /
                        zero / negative drive_id BEFORE Execute touches the DB.

P8 (eval goldens):      3 goldens at internal/ai/strategies/drive-coaching/
                        goldens.yaml + 3 canned mock responses. ai-eval --feature
                        drive-coaching → 3/3 PASS in 551.5µs.

P9 (registry coverage): All five RouteSet fields are explicit. Backend has the
                        exact route literal aivet looks for. Frontend uses the
                        actual app route /drives/:id (NOT the prompt placeholder
                        /drives/:driveId; see route registry at
                        web/src/router/routeRegistry.ts:58). UITestIDs lists the
                        wrapper's data-testid. JobNames + PushKinds are explicit
                        []string{} (CoverageOK requires non-nil arrays).

P10 (baseline coexistence): The deterministic per-drive stat cards / hero
                        gauges / energy summary on DriveDetailPage are
                        UNCONDITIONAL — they render on every code path. The AI
                        wrapper sits AFTER the stat-card block in JSX; when the
                        wrapper returns null in off mode, the surrounding stack
                        is unchanged. The Go off-mode test additionally probes
                        the GET /api/v1/drives/{driveID} baseline route under
                        the same router and asserts 200 + the
                        `surface:"baseline_stat_cards"` marker.

ADR-015 invariant mapping:
  I1  default-off:      registry entry sets DefaultOn=false; the React gate's
                        useAiEnabled('drive-coaching') reads ai_features (defaults
                        to false when the key is absent).
  I3  baseline intact:  no edit to drive_handler_detail.go or any of the
                        DriveDetailPage panels above the AI insertion point.
                        The vitest's 1st case + the Go test's 2nd probe prove
                        the baseline path is unchanged.
  I4  zero egress:      no provider call is made before guard.Wrap allows the
                        request through. The handler's first action is
                        URL-param validation; the SSE stream opens AFTER the
                        registry resolve succeeds.
  I5  hidden UI:        withAiFeature wrapper returns null when the gate is
                        closed → DOM has zero descendants of the wrapper. The
                        vitest's `expect(container).toBeEmptyDOMElement()`
                        proves this.
  I6  404 routes:       guard.Wrap returns chi's standard
                        `404 page not found\n` with no feature metadata. The
                        Go test's defence-in-depth scan rejects "drive-coaching",
                        "feature", "strategy", "provider", "coach" substrings
                        from the body.
  I7  per-feature:      gate consults BOTH ai_mode AND ai_features[id]. Vitest
                        case 2 proves a non-off mode + toggle=false also hides
                        the surface (per-feature opt-in is required, not just
                        mode!=off).
  I9  redaction:        PolicyDriveCoaching exposes ClassVehicleName only;
                        ClassLatLong / ClassStreetAddr / ClassPhone / ClassEmail /
                        ClassUserName / ClassPostal stay tagged. The strategy
                        pin-test asserts the allow-list explicitly excludes
                        ClassStreetAddr.
  I10 type system:      no JSON field is added to GET /api/v1/drives/{driveID};
                        the AI surface lives ENTIRELY under /api/v1/ai/*.
  I12 client/bg:        no new background jobs (JobNames=[]); no new push kinds
                        (PushKinds=[]); no new client storage keys; no service
                        worker chunk added by this slice (the slice prompt's
                        listed `ai-drive-coaching` chunk is left for the
                        scheduler/worker landing in a follow-up slice the same
                        way the U2 `ai_digest_weekly` worker did).

Why NeedsRAG=false (load-bearing decision; deviates from prompt suggestion):
  The slice prompt's "If this feature uses retrieval, call the single F7
  retrieval entry point for source types: drive_summary;route_efficiency;
  speed_profile" is conditional ("If"). internal/ai/rag/rag.go's source-type
  whitelist contains drive_summary but NOT route_efficiency or speed_profile.
  Adding new source types requires migrations under
  /migrations + the embeddings indexer worker — both EXPLICITLY OUTSIDE this
  slice's allowed-files list. The strategy's two read-only tools
  (query_drive_detail + query_drive_telemetry_summary) fully satisfy the
  narration's input needs from existing per-drive aggregates on *models.Drive.
  The registry entry sets NeedsRAG=false → tools/aivet's coverage check passes
  → off-mode walker has no RAG surface to enforce absence on.

Why the URL takes driveID as a chi path param (vs body):
  The AI surface attaches to a specific drive's detail page. Putting drive_id
  in the URL keeps the route shape parallel to the canonical
  GET /drives/{driveID} handler, lets `chi.URLParam` enforce presence
  declaratively, and removes the `/api/v1/ai/drives/coach` overload that a
  body-based design would require. The handler validates the param BEFORE
  opening the SSE stream so a malformed request never half-opens a stream
  that confuses the SPA.

Why the user-message in the handler does NOT include vehicle_id:
  The LLM learns the vehicle from the tool reply (query_drive_detail returns
  the *models.Drive whose VehicleID field is the authoritative source).
  Including a user-controllable vehicle hint in the prompt would give a prompt
  injection a 1-step path to cross-tenant leak (override the vehicle_id in
  the synthesised text). Keeping drive_id as the sole identifier removes
  that vector entirely.


